# Filesystem half of the bridge sandbox - rendered by install.sh
# --harden-bridge into opencode-telegram-bridge.service.d/50-hardening.conf.
#
# The bridge carries the bot token and reads Telegram's untrusted traffic.
# The process-level sandbox (no new privileges, kernel surfaces, private
# temp, capabilities, syscall and socket whitelists) lives in the bridge
# base unit itself and needs nothing from the environment. This drop-in
# adds what DOES depend on the environment: the writable boundary.
#
# It works with any configured roots, including none, because the
# bridge only writes its state, logs, heartbeat and attachment cache - all
# inside the repo directory by default (STATE_FILE/LOG_FILE parents are
# additionally granted when configured elsewhere). The project roots are
# deliberately NOT writable for the bridge; reading them stays fine through
# ProtectHome=read-only. /tmp is not granted either: the base unit runs
# with PrivateTmp=yes.
#
# Systemd unions ReadWritePaths across the base unit and its drop-ins, so
# changing the configured roots cannot add anything to the bridge: the
# grant list below is its entire writable universe. Re-run install.sh after
# changing the repo location or STATE_FILE/LOG_FILE.

[Service]
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=read-only
@RW_PATHS@
